text:
The change removes `case 'mins':` from the minutes branch of the switch (replacing it with `case "":`), but the parse regex still contains the `mins?` alternative, so inputs like `'10 mins'` still match with `match[2] === 'mins'`. After the change, `'mins'` no longer matches any case and falls through to `default: return undefined;`, so `ms('10 mins')` returns `undefined` instead of `600000`.

The new `case "":` is dead code: `type` is computed as `(match[2] || 'ms').toLowerCase()`, and `match[2]` is either a non-empty string (every regex alternative consumes at least one character) or `undefined` (coerced to `'ms'`), so it can never be `''`.

The library's own suite passes because it never exercises the `'mins'` spelling.

